# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for rcc
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* The back end

The direction is taken: c1 is rcc's only back end, it writes x86-64
and aarch64 assembly itself, and the system cc preprocesses,
assembles and links. The reasons are =docs/findings.md= and
=docs/llvm.md=, the design is =docs/backend.md= with its calls made
in =docs/design-calls.md=. The chapter holds c1's own defects on both
targets; the second target's design is =docs/aarch64.md=.

** TODO A returned ~?:~ whose arms store through a call's result moves each into the result register

A returned conditional joins in the result register: on aarch64 at
the top of the value, and below a cast, a comma or an assignment
where its arms take the name (=aarch64.md= 6.1); on x86-64 wherever
the return's name reaches it. Two arms that store through a call's
result, whose place holds that register at the store, are each moved
there: ~return c ? (*g() = a) : (*h() = b);~ is =mov w0, w1= on each
path on aarch64 and =movl %ecx, %eax= on x86-64, where a join in the
arms' register and the one move after it is an instruction fewer. On
x86-64 under a widening, ~return (long) (c ? ... : ...);~, the moves
and =movslq %eax, %rax= after the join are two instructions more than
=movslq %ecx, %rax=. =conditional.c='s =joined_called= on both targets
and =widened_called= on x86-64 have it.

Fix: none taken. The join in the arms' register is the shorter only
where both arms certainly miss the register, and whether a call's
result still holds it at the store depends on the arithmetic around
the call: ~p[f(i) + 1] = x~ holds it, ~p[i + f(j)] = x~ does not.

Check: =joined_called= with no move on its paths, and no function
longer.

** TODO aarch64: a row by a variable index on a label with no constant forms the label's address

~table[i].x~ with =x= at offset 0, and ~g[i]~ on any global array,
form the label's whole address, =adrp x0, table; add x0, x0,
:lo12:table=, then the index, and read =[x0]= or =[x0, x1, lsl #2]=.
The label row's form, the page, the index's add and =[x0,
:lo12:table]=, is one instruction fewer where the index's product is
added anyway, and the same count where the access's own form scales
the index. A row with a constant takes that form already
(=row_access()= in =src/c1-aarch64.cc=). 1230 accesses of the
=members= probe's =table_*= rows are the first kind; taken for every
global array it changes 1513 functions over =tests=, 456 shorter and
1057 at the same count with another access form.

Fix: none taken; decide its scope: records alone, rows whose index
is a product, or every global array by a variable index.

Check: the count over =tests=, and no function longer.

** TODO aarch64: a register parameter from the stack is loaded before the other parameters' stores

A =register= parameter past the eighth is loaded from the caller's
frame first, =ldr w20, [x29, #64]=, before the stores and moves of
the parameters that arrive in registers; x86-64 loads its stack
parameters in parameter order, after the six stores. =aarch64.md=
chapter 4 does not say where the load stands, and =backend.md=
chapter 4 states x86-64's order as the rule. =register-rules='s
=stacked()= pins both.

Fix: the load takes its place in parameter order, as on x86-64, and
=aarch64.md= chapter 4 says so.

Check: =stacked()= on aarch64 is pinned in parameter order, and the
chapter's sentence agrees.

** TODO A compound assignment by an identity's constant writes its operator

~x += 0~, ~x *= 1~, ~x <<= 0~ and ~p += 0~ write their operator and
their store: =addl $0, -4(%rbp)= on x86-64, =ldr; add w0, w0, #0;
str= on aarch64, and ~x /= 1~ divides. An operator whose constant
makes it the identity writes no instruction (=docs/cost.md= chapter
0), and the rule stops at a compound assignment.

Fix: a compound assignment by its operator's identity is its left
operand, generated as that expression is. The statement ~*p += 0;~
is ~*p;~: the load a discarded lvalue keeps, and no store. ~r += 0;~
on a register variable writes nothing. A call in the left operand is
made: ~p[f()] += 0;~ calls =f=. As a value, ~y = (x += 0)~, it is
the load of =x=. =cost.md= chapter 0 states the rule and the store
it does not write.

Check: a cost program with each form, pinned on both targets, and no
function longer.

** TODO aarch64: a narrow or widened prefix step's value is stepped outside the register variable

~r = ++c~ on a =char= in memory into a ~register int r~ is =ldrb w0,
[x29, #24]; add w0, w0, #1; strb w0, [x29, #24]; uxtb w19, w0=, and
~l = ++x~ on an =int= into a =register long= ends =sxtw x19, w0=:
the step is in an expression register, and the conversion's
extension writes the variable. The load and the step in the
variable, =ldrb w19; add w19, w19, #1; strb w19; uxtb w19, w19=, is
the same count and takes no expression register, but the variable's
register then holds a value outside its representation from the add
to the extension, which chapter 4 of =aarch64.md= does not allow
today. 23 forms over =tests=; x86-64 keeps the same split.

Fix: none taken; it moves the cast's rule on what a register
variable's register may hold.

Check: the count over =tests=, and no function longer.

** TODO A 32-bit write's value widened as an unsigned is cleared again

Any write of a 32-bit register name clears the upper half, so the
value is valid to 64 bits as an =unsigned=. A signed value no load
wrote keeps its clearing move all the same: ~(long) (unsigned)
narrow()~ on a call's ~signed char~ result is =movsbl %al, %eax; movl
%eax, %eax= and =sxtb w0, w0; mov w0, w0= (=unsigned_called()= of
=widen=); =addl $3, %eax; movl %eax, %eax= is written 4 times in
=test_writes= of =tests/probe/statics-main.c= on each target, with 3
joins beside them; and on x86-64 a =switch= on a 32-bit sum clears
its table's index, =addl $1, %eax; cmpl $4, %eax; ja; movl %eax,
%eax=, 20 sites over =tests=. The record of a value's valid width is
by its type's signedness, so a signed =int= valid to 32 bits is a
32-bit write of the generator's, a 64-bit value narrowed, which
writes nothing, or a call's result, whose upper half the convention
leaves undefined, and a conversion to =unsigned= cannot tell them
apart. ~(long) (unsigned) whole()~ on an =int= call must keep its
move (=unsigned_int_called()= of =widen=).

Fix: none taken. It needs a record of its own on a held value, that
its register's upper half is zero: set by every 32-bit write, lost
at a call's result, a narrowing and a 64-bit write, kept by a spill
and its reload, true past a join only where every path has it, and
never a register variable's register. That touches most rows of both
generators for the sites above.

Check: those sites with no clearing move, =unsigned_int_called()=
with its own, and no function longer.

** TODO The walk under the casts that keep the bits is written four times

=object_under()= in =src/c1-common.cc=, and =memory_operand()=,
=register_operand()= and =under_kept_bits()= in =src/c1-x86-64.cc=
each step through the casts =same_bits()= accepts, in a loop of
their own.

Fix: one function in =src/c1-common.cc=, called by the four.

Check: no listing over =tests= changes.

* Guarantees

The promises in =docs/guarantees.md=. The entry number is the
guarantee it belongs to. All are decided; G15 is withdrawn with its
number reserved. c1 writes none of the guard of G4, the round trip
of G8 and the laundering of G16, which draft 1 designed against an
optimizer, because nothing between the source and the instruction
reasons about the program.

** TODO G3's "the count as written" does not hold on aarch64

G3's instruction sentence says a shift is "by =cl= or by an
immediate, the count as written". On aarch64 the generator masks an
immediate count to the width, since the instruction's immediate
cannot hold more: ~x << 33~ on an =int= is =lsl w0, w0, #1=, as the
shift's row of =docs/aarch64.md= says. x86-64 writes =shll $33,
%eax=, and the machine masks it.

Fix: G3 says the count is written as it stands on x86-64 and masked
to the width on aarch64.

Check: G3 and the row of =aarch64.md= agree with the pin of
=add-zero='s =shifted_past()= on each target.

* Cost model

The record in =docs/cost.md=: what each construct compiles to under
c1, its table written from the pins of =tests/cost/=. The findings of
the measurement (=docs/findings.md=) were facts about LLVM's code
generator and left with it; that page's chapter 8 says what became
of each. The entries below are rows the table lacks.

** TODO Chapter 4's x86-64 numbers predate the listings of its programs

The x86-64 table of =cost.md= chapter 4 was measured before a
remainder's value became the value in =rdx=. That step takes one
instruction, =movq %rdx, %rax=, out of the step loop of ~rcc
collatz.c~, the loop the cell times: 2293 ms, 7.75. The other
listings of =tests/perf= it changes, it changes outside their hot
loops, and the loops of =tests/slot= are the same. The operand in
memory taken by a multiply by a constant and by a cast of
signedness changes every x86-64 listing of =tests/perf= again, by
one to five instructions, outside the loops the chapter times: the
step of each program's generator and its checksum. An address
written into a =register= variable's register takes one =movq= out
of =matmul-tuned= before its inner loop, whose head is then at
0x1320, in the second half of its 64-byte line. The =leaq= of ~pb
+= n~ written into the variable's register takes an instruction out
of that loop, which is nine instructions and 29 bytes; the chapter
states the listing of today, and its matmul cell, 572 ms, and the
rows of 4.2, which names a loop of 32 bytes, are of the loop of ten. A
constant on the left taken as one on the right shortens the step
loop of collatz on x86-64, as written by one instruction, =imulq $3,
-48(%rbp), %rax=, and as tuned by two, =imulq $3, %r14, %r14; addq
$1, %r14=: the cells 2293 and 473 ms are of the longer loops. A
multiply of a =register= variable by a constant, now the one
three-operand =imul=, takes an instruction out of the checksum loop
of ~rcc strings-tuned.c~, =imull $31, %ebx, %eax=, which runs once
for each string.

A sum whose right operand holds a call, written into the register
its consumer names, takes one instruction out of the path of ~rcc
fib.c~ that recurses, which the fib cell times.

Fix: =make bench= three times on the machine of the table, and the
table and what is drawn from it taken again.

Check: the table is of a run made after the last change to a hot
loop of its programs.

** TODO The Oryon's numbers of chapter 4 predate the listings of its programs

The Oryon's table of =cost.md= chapter 4, its column in 4.3 and in
=slot.md=, and the aarch64 alignment probe of 4.2, on the Oryon and
on Firestorm, were measured on 2026-11-13. The aarch64 listings of
collatz and strings as written, and of collatz, matmul, sieve, sort
and strings as tuned, have changed since; the slot loops have not.
The chapter says so under the table. The sentence of 4.3 that the
Zen 2 slot times keep their ratios to =rr='s within 1% with the boost
on was measured before the x86-64 loops of =sr= and =ss= changed.

Fix: =make bench= three times on the Oryon; the aarch64 probe of 4.2
again on both cores; one ~make slots CORE=1~ on Zen 2 with the boost
on.

Check: every cell of those tables and lists is of a run made after
the last change to its listing.

** TODO The M1 Max's fib cell of chapter 4 predates its listing

The M1 Max's table of =cost.md= chapter 4 was measured before a
=return='s value was computed in =x0= on aarch64. That takes one
instruction, =mov w0, w1=, out of the path of ~rcc fib.c~ that
recurses, 24 instructions where the cell timed 25. =fib-tuned= and
the other programs' timed loops are the same.

Fix: =make bench= three times on the M1 Max, and the fib row and
what is drawn from it taken again.

Check: the cell is of a run made after the last change to fib's
listing.

* Tests

** TODO =tests/slots= reads the clock of an idle core

The script reads =scaling_cur_freq= once, before the first timed
run, and computes the cycle column at that frequency. The core is
idle then. On a core with a frequency boost the reading is not the
clock a run gets: on the Zen 2 core with the boost on it read 3853
and 3593 MHz in two series, and =rr= came out at 0.84 and 0.78
cycles an iteration, under the one cycle its add takes. The script
prints the column all the same; the only sign is that the reading
after the last run differs, which its header mentions and its output
does not flag.

Fix: read the frequency while a run is in flight, or leave the cycle
columns out and say why when the two readings differ by more than a
percent.

Check: on an x86-64 core with its boost on, =make slots= prints =rr=
at about one cycle an iteration, or prints no cycle column and says
why.

** TODO Fenced text in the tests keeps an indentation of 4

The C sources of =tests/= are laid out by =clang-format=, indented
by 8. What the formatter cannot read, a list of macro invocations
side by side or a table it would write one element a line, stands
between =/* clang-format off */= and =/* clang-format on */=, 84
places in 32 files, and keeps the layout it had: continuation lines
indented by 4, the backslash at its old column.

Fix: the fenced text indented by 8 by rule, its backslash column
moved with it.

Check: no fenced line of =tests/= begins with exactly 4 spaces, and
every listing is byte-identical.

** TODO No run checks the width of an access to a global or a local

The =bounds= probe makes an access wider than its object fault where
the object is reached through a pointer, a member's form among them:
it places the object against an unmapped page. A global's and a
local's addresses are the program's to place, so a read of 4 bytes
of a =char= global or local passes every run, for the reason the
probe was written. Their widths are seen only by the comparison of
listings against the tree before a change, access by access, which
is no part of =make test=, and by a reader of the cost pins.

Fix: none taken; a check of each access's width in the listings of
=tests= against the type the source gives the object, as part of
=make test=.

Check: a compiler that reads a =char= local at 4 bytes fails =make
test=.

** TODO No test copies a record aligned to 16, or runs the copy's loop at a unit of 8

A record with a ~long double~ member is aligned to 16 and copied in
units of 8 on both targets; no cost program and no probe holds one,
and a copy in units of the alignment, which moved 4 bytes of each 16
on aarch64, passed every test. The =copies= probe's largest record
aligned to 8 is of eight units, which is written out, so neither
target's copy loop is run at a unit of 8.

Fix: such a record in =struct-copy.c= and in the =copies= probe;
=a8_72= and =a8_200= among the probe's sized records.

Check: a compiler that copies in units of 16 fails =make test= on
aarch64, and the probe's count rises with 0 differ on both targets.

* Parser

** TODO =always_inline= marks

A function declared =always_inline= is "not yet: attribute
'always_inline'" at its use or its definition. rcc inlines nothing,
and =gcc= and =clang= make the attribute an error where they cannot
inline the call; rcc writing the call is what a compiler at =-O0=
does for a function not declared inline.

Fix: dropped, with a warning that rcc inlines nothing: the call is
written.

Check: a cost program with the call, and the warning pinned.

** TODO A static or a weak alias is not yet

~static int a(void) __attribute__((alias("t")));~ and
~__attribute__((weak, alias("t")))~ are "not yet" where declared;
=clang= writes the first without =.globl= and the second with =.weak=
in its place.

Fix: write them; a static alias counts as its symbol's definition
for 6.9.1p3.

Check: a cost program with both forms, pinned on each target.

** TODO Arithmetic on a pointer to =void= is an error

~p + 1~ on a ~void *~ fails with "arithmetic on a pointer to void":
6.5.7p2 and p3 ask a pointer to a complete object type. GNU C takes
it, the pointee's size 1, and a program written for =gcc= and
=clang= may have it.

Fix: taken as GNU C takes it, with a warning: the sum, the
difference and the step count bytes, as on a ~char *~. ~sizeof
(void)~ stays an error.

Check: a cost program pinned like its ~char *~ twin on both targets,
and the warning pinned.

** TODO ~return f();~ of a void =f= in a void function is an error

It fails with "void function should not return a value": 6.8.7.5p1
allows a void function no =return= with an expression. GNU C takes a
void expression there.

Fix: taken with a warning where the expression is void: the
expression for its effect, then the return. An expression of a value
stays an error.

Check: a cost program pinned like ~f(); return;~ on both targets,
and the warning pinned.

** TODO A ~void *~ and a pointer to a function convert only under a cast

~void (*fp)(void) = p;~ with =p= a ~void *~ fails with "incompatible
pointer types": 6.5.17.2p1 lets a ~void *~ meet a pointer to an
object type alone. GNU C takes it, in both directions.

Fix: taken with a warning, in both directions. It has no instruction
of its own: the address is moved as under the cast.

Check: a cost program pinned like its twin with the cast written,
and the warning pinned.

** TODO A statement expression, ~a ?: b~ and =alloca= are refused in an error's words

~({ ... })~ and ~a ?: b~ fail with "expected expression", and
=alloca= with "'__builtin_alloca' undeclared": the program is GNU C
that rcc does not compile yet, and the message calls it wrong.

Fix: each is taken, in this order: the statement expression, a block
whose last expression is its value; ~a ?: b~, =a= evaluated once; a
variable length array; =alloca=. Until one is built, its refusal
says "not yet" and names it.

Check: a program of =tests/error= for each until it compiles, then a
cost program on both targets.

** TODO Assignment to a const object is not diagnosed

The parser accepts =const= and drops it: the type is the machine's
view and carries no qualifier. So =const int c = 1; c = 2;= compiles,
though 6.5.17.1p2 requires a modifiable lvalue and 6.3.2.1p1 excludes
a const-qualified one; a conforming compiler must diagnose it. The
same silence covers a =const= pointee assigned through, and a
=T *= initialized from a =const T *=. =volatile= is dropped the same
way, and a =T *= initialized from a =volatile T *= is not diagnosed
either, though 6.5.17.2p1 asks that the left pointee have every
qualifier of the right.

Fix: qualifiers on the symbol and on the pointee, checked by
=require_modifiable_lvalue= and by the assignment conversion; the
type itself stays unqualified.

Check: the three programs above fail with a diagnostic; =const= in
every other position still parses.

** TODO A pointer to a type rcc does not honour takes no pointer to the type behind it

~typedef int word_t __attribute__ ((mode (word))); void f(word_t *);
void g(int *p) { f(p); }~ fails with "incompatible pointer types";
so does the same program with ~typedef _Atomic int a_t;~. The error
calls the program invalid where rcc has not looked: a typedef through
a marking attribute or =_Atomic= names a type of an identity of its
own, which keeps nothing of the type it was made from.

Fix: the refusal says "not yet" and names the typedef's words, where
one of the two pointees is such a type; or the type keeps what it was
made from.

Check: the first program is refused as not yet, or compiles.

** TODO A type rcc does not honour cannot be written twice

~typedef int w __attribute__ ((mode (word))); typedef int w
__attribute__ ((mode (word)));~ fails with "redefinition of typedef
'w' with another type". A typedef may be declared again as the same
type, and these two are. Each declaration through an attribute rcc
does not honour makes a type of an identity of its own, so the
second is never the first. Three more are made the same way and fail
the same way: a typedef through =_Atomic=; the atomic type specifier,
so that ~void f(_Atomic(int) *); void f(_Atomic(int) *);~ fails with
"conflicting types for 'f'"; and a typedef of an array whose size rcc
cannot compute, ~typedef int a[sizeof (struct s)];~ written twice,
=s= a struct with a bit-field.

Fix: a type made again by the same means from the same type takes
the earlier one's identity.

Check: the programs above compile; with =mode (byte)= in the second
typedef the first still fails.

** TODO The other target is preprocessed with this machine's headers

=rcc -target x86-64= on an aarch64 machine runs =clang -E
--target=x86_64-linux-gnu=, which searches =/usr/include=: the
aarch64 glibc's headers, read with x86-64's predefined macros. For
=stdio.h= the two texts are the same here, so the pin of
=header-stdio= for the other target is honest; a header that differs
by architecture, =bits/= and =asm/= above all, would give the other
target this machine's declarations.

Fix: say in =shape.md= chapter 8 that a program with system headers
is compiled for the machine's own target, and refuse =-target= for
another machine when the program includes one; or take a sysroot.

Check: the documents and the driver agree on what =-target= does to
=#include <...>=.

** TODO A call through a pointer passes a parameter a marking attribute marked

~int f(int x __attribute__ ((mode (word))));~ marks =f=, and a call
of it is refused, "not yet: attribute 'mode'": rcc would pass the
argument as an =int= where the attribute asks for another size. The
same parameter in a pointer's prototype is not refused: ~int call(int
(*p)(int x __attribute__ ((mode (word))))) { return p(1); }~ compiles,
and the call passes an =int=. The mark is the symbol's; a function
type has none, so the attribute is lost on a pointer's prototype and
on a typedef of function type.

Fix: the function type carries the marking, so that a call through a
pointer to it is refused as a call of a marked function is.

Check: the second program above is refused by the attribute's name.

** TODO An error after the first not-yet in a typedef's array size is not seen

~typedef int a[sizeof (struct s) + undeclared];~, =s= a struct with a
bit-field, compiles: in a
typedef, an array size that needs what rcc does not have yet makes a
type rcc declares and does not use, and the parse of the size ends at
its first refusal, so the undeclared name after it is never read.

Fix: the rest of the size is parsed for its errors after the refusal;
or the documents say that a size rcc cannot compute is not checked.

Check: the typedef above fails with "'undeclared' undeclared".

** TODO A parameter of a typedef's array type rcc cannot size is not a pointer

With =s= a struct with a bit-field,
~typedef long g_t[sizeof (struct s)]; void f(g_t g); void f(long
*g);~ fails with "conflicting types for 'f'". A parameter of array
type is adjusted to a pointer to the element, and =g_t= is an array
of =long=; but the typedef names a type rcc does not use, which is
no array in the tree, so nothing is adjusted. glibc declares no such
parameter in the headers surveyed.

Fix: the type keeps its element, so that a parameter of it is
adjusted.

Check: the program above compiles.

** TODO =_Atomic ( type-name )= takes an array and a function type

~typedef _Atomic(int [3]) a_t;~ and ~typedef _Atomic(int (void))
f_t;~ are accepted. 6.7.3.5p3 says the type name "shall not refer to
an array type, a function type, an atomic type, or a qualified
type". The specifier makes a type rcc declares and does not use and
looks at nothing inside the parentheses; a qualified type cannot be
told, since qualifiers are dropped.

Fix: the specifier refuses an array and a function type.

Check: two programs of =tests/error= with their pins.

** TODO A macro of =stdatomic.h= draws "undeclared"

~atomic_flag_test_and_set(p)~ fails with "'__c11_atomic_exchange'
undeclared": the header's macros expand to =clang='s builtin
functions, which rcc takes for identifiers. The program is valid, and
the message says a name is missing where an atomic access is not
yet.

Fix: a name that begins =__c11_atomic_= or =__atomic_= is refused as
not yet, by name.

Check: the call above fails with "not yet", pinned in =tests/error=.

** TODO A qualified =void= is taken as the =void= of an empty parameter list

~int f(const void);~, ~int f(void const);~ and a parameter of a
typedef for ~const void~ declare a function of no parameters. The
special case is "an unnamed parameter of type void" (6.7.7.4p9), and
a qualified =void= is a distinct type (6.2.5p31), whose use there
J.2 (75) leaves undefined; =clang= refuses it. rcc drops a qualifier
where it reads it, so it cannot tell. A storage class on that =void=
is refused.

Fix: with the qualifiers the entry on =const= keeps on the symbol, a
typedef's included.

Check: the three declarations fail with "'void' must be the only
parameter".

** TODO =[*]= in brackets other than the outermost is "expected expression"

~void f(int a[3][*]);~ and ~void f(int n, int (*a)[*]);~ are valid
prototypes (6.7.7.3p4, 6.7.7.4p11), which =clang= takes, and fail
with "expected expression": =[*]= is read in the brackets a
parameter is adjusted from and nowhere else. ~int x[*];~ and
~typedef int t[*];~ draw the same words, where the error is of the
star outside a prototype.

Fix: in a parameter declaration's other brackets =[*]= is a variable
length array, a not-yet as =[n]= is there; outside a parameter
declaration it is an error in words of its own. The parser must know
it is inside a parameter declaration, not only right after the name.

Check: the two prototypes say "not yet: variable length array" at
the =*=, and ~int x[*];~ is pinned in =tests/error= with its message.

** TODO An incomplete record of internal linkage is refused where it is declared

~static struct S s; struct S { int x; };~ is refused at the first
declaration, "incomplete type", where an array of unknown size of
internal linkage is judged at the unit's end, by the type the unit's
declarations give it together, and =clang= takes both. 6.9.3p3 asks
that a tentative definition of internal linkage not be of an
incomplete type, and says nothing of when.

Fix: a record follows the array: judged at the unit's end, its
object made once the record is complete.

Check: the program above compiles, and one whose record is never
completed is an error at the unit's end, pinned.

** TODO A header's =static inline= definition under a marking attribute is not remembered as a definition

A system header's =static inline= definition whose specifiers carry
a marking attribute, ~static inline __attribute__((__always_inline__))
int f(int x) { ... }~, leaves the attribute's words on the function,
not the words of a skipped body. A second definition in the header
is then accepted with no diagnostic, and one in the program draws
"not yet: attribute 'always_inline'"; =clang= says "redefinition of
'f'" for both (6.9.1p3).

Fix: the skipped definition is recorded apart from the mark, a flag
of the function's, and =is_defined()= asks it.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with the header's two definitions,
pinned "redefinition of 'f'".

** TODO Pointers to two integer types of one representation convert with no diagnostic

~long *p; long long *q = p;~ compiles, and so does an assignment of
an ~enum a *~ to an ~enum b *~ where both have one integer type:
=long= and =long long= are one type in the tree, and an enumerated
type is its integer type (=shape.md= chapter 3). 6.5.17.2p1 asks
compatible pointee types, and =clang= rejects both programs.

Fix: both are errors, and the cast is the program's to write. An
integer type carries its identity for the check of compatibility,
which =_Generic= needs of it too, so the two are built together.

Check: both programs fail with "incompatible pointer types", and
compile with the cast written.

** TODO A local array's list, a designator and a string for a member array are not yet

A local record takes its initializer list (G20). Three forms beside
it are refused by name: a local array's list, an array of records'
among them, "not yet: array initializer"; a designator, ~.x = 1~ and
~[2] = 3~, in any list, "not yet: designator"; and a string literal
for a member array of =char= in a local's list, whose bytes need a
copy's row that arrays do not have.

Fix: none taken; each is built when a program needs it, the array's
list on the record's zeroing and stores.

Check: a cost program for each form on both targets, and the
=zeroing= probe with a local array.

** TODO A bit-field or an anonymous member after a flexible array member is accepted

~struct s { int n; int a[]; int x : 3; };~ compiles, and so does an
unnamed bit-field or an anonymous struct after =a=: 6.7.3.2p3 lets
only the last member be a flexible array member, and =clang= says
"flexible array member 'a' ... is not at the end". A named member
after it is refused; =bit_field()= and =unnamed_member()= in
=src/parse.cc= do not look.

Fix: both refuse as =add_member()= does.

Check: a program of =tests/error= for each form, pinned "not at the
end".

** TODO A record with a flexible array member is taken as a member and as an element

~struct h { int n; char b[]; }; struct o { int k; struct h x; };
struct h arr[3];~ compiles with no diagnostic, laid out as =clang=
lays it out. 6.7.3.2p3 says such a structure "shall not be a member
of a structure or an element of an array"; GNU C takes it.

Fix: an error in the program's own text; in a system header's text
it is laid out with no word.

Check: a program of =tests/error= for the member and for the
element.

** TODO A not-yet of c1 in an uncalled =static inline= body stops the file

A =static inline= body that stops at a not-yet of the parser's is
kept as a function not yet compiled (=docs/shape.md= chapter 5). A
construct the parser takes and a generator refuses is not caught: an
uncalled =static inline= function whose body holds one is "rcc:
internal error: c1: not yet: ..." for every file that includes its
header.

Fix: the generator's refusal of such a function is kept as the
parser's is; or the parser refuses the construct itself.

Check: a program whose uncalled =static inline= function holds a
construct only c1 refuses compiles.

** TODO A not-yet in the head of an uncalled =static inline= definition stops the file

~static inline struct p f(void) { ... }~, never called, is "not yet:
struct 'p' returned by value": the refusal is in the result type,
outside the body, where the parser does not catch it. A parameter's
type is the same.

Fix: the catch that keeps a body uncompiled covers the definition's
head.

Check: that program compiles, and a call of =f= is refused by the
head's words.

** TODO A function declared in the declaration of a =for= is taken

~for (int f(void); ;)~ compiles with no diagnostic. C17's 6.8.5p3,
which =shape.md= cites for the =static= refused there, allows only
objects of storage class =auto= or =register=; C23 allows the
function, and the dialect is C17's.

Fix: refused as =static= is, an error.

Check: the program is pinned in =tests/error=.

** TODO A constant expression out of its type's range wraps with no diagnostic

6.6p4 is a constraint: "Each constant expression shall evaluate to a
constant that is in the range of representable values for its type."
Where the grammar requires a constant expression, an array's size, a
=case= label, an enumerator, a static initializer and
=_Static_assert=, the parser takes the wrapped value silently, as G1
makes the same arithmetic wrap at run time: ~int a[(2147483647 + 1)
/ 4 + 600000000];~ has 63129088 elements. =clang= warns in each. The
comment on the evaluator in =src/parse.cc=, "the wrapped value is
refused there", says what the documents no longer say.

Fix: none taken; decide: a diagnostic where a constant expression is
required, or the documents' sentence that rcc gives none. The
comment follows either.

Check: ~case 2147483647 + 1:~ draws the diagnostic, or =shape.md=
says why not; the comment agrees.

** TODO A marking attribute after the definition is taken

~int f(void) { return 0; } int f(void) __attribute__ ((weak));~
compiles, and =f= is strong. =gcc= writes =.weak f=; =clang= ignores
the attribute, with a warning. An attribute rcc does not honour is
refused after a use of the symbol; a definition is not counted as
one.

Fix: a definition counts as a use.

Check: that program is refused at the attribute, pinned.

* Lexer

** TODO ~#pragma GCC visibility push(default)~ is refused

Every =#pragma GCC visibility= is "not yet", the push of =default=
among them. Default visibility is what rcc writes, so a region under
it is translated as the program asks, and a header that brackets its
declarations with ~push(default)~ and =pop=, the compiler's own
=unwind.h= among them, is refused for nothing.

Fix: the lexer keeps the visibility in force, as it keeps a packing,
and a declaration made under one that is not =default= is the
not-yet.

Check: a program that includes =unwind.h= compiles, and the pin of
=pragma-visibility= is refused at its declaration.

